raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does a Democratic campaign ad labeling Trump a convicted criminal change how Democrats judge people with criminal records who were not the target of the ad?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Thus, this study explores what may have been the impact of propagating such labels on Democrats’ perceptions of\npeople with criminal records,",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does the ad change how well Democrats feel their party represents them and whether they see elected officials as serving the public rather than themselves?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well\nDemocrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "their perceptions of elected officials’ motivations\n(i.e., the extent to which they are motivated by serving the public, as opposed to their own self-interest.)",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary question is whether hearing a formerly incarcerated legislator’s perspective before seeing the ad changes Democrats’ response to it, including their support for using it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the\nprimary purpose of this study was to test whether providing a counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump\nadvertisement significantly impacted how Democrats responded to the advertisement.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "will significantly decrease support for the use of the advertisement, on average, relative to exposure to\nthe advertisement of Trump being labeled as a “convicted criminal” alone.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A secondary question is whether the treatments affect Democrats differently when they or a loved one have been involved in the justice system.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "supplemental analyses will also explore potential heterogeneity by\nsystem-impact status (i.e., whether someone identifies as having previously been involved in the system or impacted by\nthe system through their loved one’s involvement.)",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The ad produced a trade-off: Democrats felt better represented by their party, but the deterioration in their views of people with records was substantially larger than the gain in perceived representation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Results suggest that exposure\nto the Trump advertisement with the “convicted criminal” label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records, but improved\nperceived feelings of representation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "the magnitude of these effects (approximately 0.30 to 0.34 in absolute value SD units) are considerably smaller than\nthe advertisement’s effects on perceptions of individuals with criminal records (approximately 0.83)",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The legislator’s account nearly offset the harm to people with records. Harney therefore argues for testing campaign messages that acknowledge Trump’s convictions while giving formerly incarcerated representatives a voice, rather than treating a stigmatizing attack as necessary.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "but when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated,\nsystem-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the “criminal” label, these effects were almost entirely\nameliorated",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Instead, a viable, alternate route for political messaging\nduring the 2024 presidential election could have been to acknowledge, but juxtapose, the nature of Trump’s convictions and his\nbroadcasted attitude about his convictions with effective counter-narratives.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study recruited 2,500 English-speaking U.S. Democrats on Prolific on July 12–13, 2025. After checking attention, identifiers, completion codes and likely bot responses, 2,439 participants remained.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "2,500 participants were\nrecruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025 [60]. This survey was fielded amongst individuals residing within\nthe United States who spoke fluent English and were members of the Democratic Party.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "2,481 were retained after dropping those who failed the attention check,\nwere duplicate observations based on stored Prolific identifier embedded data, or provided the wrong completion code",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Approximately 1.69% of responses post-removal of duplicates, incorrect/no codes, and failed attention checks were\nflagged as not being a likely human response, and were also dropped. No observations were flagged as coming from the\nsame IP addressing, leaving the remaining, final analytical sample size of 2,439 participants.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The experiment varied the order of exposure. Controls answered the main outcomes before either stimulus; a second group answered after the ad alone; a third answered after the narrative and then the ad. All groups rated the ad immediately after seeing it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the\nControl group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "The “control group” is specifically\nthose who were assigned to answer all outcome questions first (with exception of the advertisement support question,) while “Trump Advertisement (Ad.)\nAlone” were those who were assigned to be presented with the Trump advertisement first.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "the Counter-Narrative First group presented the Trump advertisement (and its associated questions) only after being shown the counter-narrative. Then the outcomes were presented.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "the advertisement support question, which is asked immediately after being presented with the advertisement itself.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad stimulus was an image used in the 2024 campaign: Trump’s mugshot overlaid with the words CONVICTED CRIMINAL. The image and accompanying campaign context were held constant.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Regardless of when participants were presented with the Trump advertisement, the content was (importantly) constant\nacross treatment arms.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Below this descriptive text, participants were presented with one of the images\nutilized in advertisements within the 2024 presidential election, specifically President Donald J. Trump’s mugshot, which\nwas labeled, in all capital letters, “CONVICTED CRIMINAL.”",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The counterexample was an edited news excerpt about Nevada legislator Jovan Jackson. It described his reform work and why he found the campaign’s contrast between a prosecutor and a felon hurtful; it distinguished rehabilitation from Trump’s response to conviction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Take for example, Jovan Jackson, an\nassemblymember (D), and likely, the first formerly incarcerated person to serve as a state legislator in Nevada.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "The text was lightly edited for\nbrevity, to remove mention of the state in which this individual is an elected official, and their first name, to more narrowly\ndefine the counter-narrative treatment.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Jackson found the contrast hurtful and undercut the work he had done since being incarcerated. He said there is a clear difference between a formerly incarcerated person who sought rehabilitation, served\ntheir time and was released, and Trump.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Six outcomes distinguished attitudes toward people with records, the value of diverse life experiences in office, whether that value depends on effective advocacy, the quality of federal Democratic representation, officials’ motives and support for the ad. Value and complexity were single items; the other four measures were standardized indices.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The primary outcomes of interest in this study were: a) perceptions of individuals with criminal records; b) perceived value\nof representation; c) perceived quality of Democratic representation in federal government; d) complexity of representation; e) motives of elected officials; and f) advertisement support.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Having representatives\nwith a wide array of life experiences is really only beneficial when our representatives effectively advocate for the needs of\ntheir constituents.”",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The remaining four outcomes are measured as indices, averaged over each question included in the index, and subsequently standardized for ease of interpretation.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Two preregistered contrasts estimated average treatment effects: narrative followed by ad versus ad alone, and ad alone versus control. Harney used least squares with HC-2 robust standard errors and reported estimates with and without prespecified covariates.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the first (Hypothesis Set 1) corresponds with testing the impact of exposure to\nperspective-getting counter-narrative immediately before exposure to the Trump ad, relative to only being presented\nwith the Trump ad. The second set (Hypothesis Set 2) includes tests of the advertisement’s impact, relative to no\nexposure to the advertisement prior to answering outcome questions.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "To estimate each ATE, least squares regressions with heteroskedasticity-consistent standard error\nestimators, HC-2, were utilized. Both the treatment indicator only and the primary models (which include covariates, as\npre-specified), are reported for transparency.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The author checked balance across the groups being compared and used a reported emotional reaction to Jackson’s account as a manipulation check. Participants who read it first reported a stronger reaction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "there is no statistical evidence of imbalance between treatment arms that are being compared to one another\nin this study.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "the\nCounter-Narrative First group exhibited significantly greater emotional reaction to the narrative than either of the other\ntwo treatment arms, which were primed with the Trump advertisement before the counter-narrative,",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Supplemental analyses examined the individual attitude items, demographic predictors and treatment differences by justice-system involvement. Of the respondents, 521 identified themselves as system-impacted.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "on individual items for the perceptions of individuals with criminal records index, given its marginally acceptable reliability\ncoefficient; b) summarization of the sociodemographic factors that were significantly predictive of the outcomes of interest;\nand c) heterogeneity in results by system-impact status.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "whether someone identified themselves as being systemimpacted (n = 521) or not (n = 1,918)",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Compared with answering before seeing the ad, seeing it first lowered the attitude index for people with records by 0.835 standard deviations. The largest item-level declines concerned whether people with records contribute to their communities and whether a record defines a person’s character.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044)",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "c) having criminal records is not a strong reflection of someone’s\noverall character ( β̂0 = −1.018, SE = 0.043) and ( β̂2 = 0.945, SE = 0.043); and d) disagreeing that most people with criminal records do not contribute positively to their communities ( β̂0 = −1.251, SE = 0.041) and ( β̂2 = 1.183, SE = 0.040).",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Compared with the ad alone, reading Jackson’s account first raised positive attitudes toward people with records by about 0.83 standard deviations and the perceived democratic value of diverse life experiences in office by 0.79 standard deviations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump advertisement increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation (SD) units, compared to those\npresented with the Trump advertisement alone",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "the initial exposure to the counter-narrative demonstrating a 0.79 SD unit increase in the perceived value of diversity\nof life experience as an important component of strengthening democracy, relative to those only presented with the Trump\nadvertisement before answering outcome questions",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Ratings of the party moved differently from ratings of people with records. The ad alone made Democrats feel better represented; the narrative before the ad lowered ratings of federal Democratic representation by about 0.33 standard deviations relative to the ad alone. The narrative did not significantly change whether respondents thought representation also required effective advocacy.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement significantly increased feelings of being well represented by elected officials",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased quality of Democratic representation in federal government, relative to just Trump advertisement exposure ( β̂2 = −0.332, SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "there were\nno significant differences between the Counter-Narrative First and Trump Ad Alone groups on the outcome related to the\ncomplexity of representation",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Hearing Jackson first reduced support for using the ad. When the two items in the support index were examined separately, endorsement of the ad declined significantly, but belief in its effectiveness did not.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement, relative to the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = −0.172 SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Both coefficients are negative,\nincluding that of the item “This is an effective advertisement” and “I support the use of this advertisement”, though only the\nexplicit support for the advertisement item was statistically significant.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Respondents affected by the justice system generally held more favorable views of people with records, yet the ad’s negative effects on several attitude items were larger for them, not smaller.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "those who identified as being system-impacted reported significantly more positive perceptions overall and within each\ntreatment arm, with only one exception.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the Trump advertisement exposure appeared to be significantly more damaging\nto perceptions of individuals with criminal records for those who are system-impacted.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The sample addresses the party’s own constituents. It does not establish how Republicans, independents or the electorate as a whole would respond.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the purpose of the study is to understand the impacts of Democratic Party advertising on feelings of being\nadequately represented and related outcomes, thus the study sample focused only on their constituents.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Budget constraints ruled out a narrative-only arm. The experiment identifies what changes when the narrative precedes the ad, not what Jackson’s story would do on its own or whether the negative ad modifies its effect.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the counter-narrative arm cannot be directly compared to the pure control arm to test the\nimpact of the counter-narrative alone because it also involves exposure to the Trump advertisement.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "utilizing a negative campaign advertisement could moderate the effect of the counter-narrative perspective intervention,\ndepending upon one’s personality traits, amongst other potential factors [76].",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Because the mugshot and label were always shown together, the study cannot attribute the ad’s effect to the wording rather than the image.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from\nthe stigmatizing terminology imposed, and thus, this is recommended for exploration in future work.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The results do not settle whether Jackson’s example reduced stigma generally or encouraged respondents to classify some people with records as more deserving than others.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While it is possible that the intervention constructs a new category of deservingness, rather than reducing stigma, the\nempirical relationship between attitudes and personal connection suggests that reduction of stigma is more likely [55–57].\nStill, future research should clarify this relationship more deeply.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Screening did not eliminate uncertainty about AI-generated responses in an online panel; the extent of AI use in closed-ended surveys is not known.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "It is also incredibly important to acknowledge that this survey was fielded amongst an online panel of survey respondents, and the proliferation of generative AI should cause concerns over the humanity of the responses provided within\nthe survey.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "the propensity for the standard online survey taker to utilize AI agents to\ncomplete close-ended surveys is not well understood.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Participants may have inferred the study’s purpose and given socially desirable answers. The emotional-reaction check does not exclude that explanation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "it could be the case that participants were subject to the Hawthorne effect, resulting in\noutcomes being driven – in some capacity – by social desirability bias.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "helps alleviate some\nconcerns but does not rule this out.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A single survey session cannot establish how long the effects last or how they change with repeated exposure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative\nprime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist; b) how may effects vary by single versus repeated\nexposure to the advertisement and/or the counter-narrative prime?",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The outcomes are reported attitudes, not actions. The experiment does not show that either message changes votes, turnout or other decisions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent would these treatments translate to shifted decision-making and behavior, as opposed to self-reported perceptions and attitudes?",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Respondents already knew the election result. Effects measured in July 2025 need not match those of seeing the ad during the 2024 campaign.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this current study was conducted in July of 2025, after the outcome of the election was known and not actively\nduring the campaign. Whether these results replicate during campaign periods is an open question, perhaps especially so\nfor the advertisement’s impacts in particular.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The attitude index had only marginally adequate reliability. In particular, the item about whether the label itself causes harm moved opposite to expectation and may not measure respondents’ views of people with records.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the Cronbach’s alpha coefficient was 0.71, reflecting the lower end of the threshold\nfor adequate reliability [59].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "It could be that the\nquestion was misinterpreted, that solely providing the Trump advertisement as a stimulus reduces the extent to which\nparticipants embraced nuance, and/or that this is item measures something more distinct from perceptions of individuals\nwith records.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The representation measures do not cover all the ways constituents can feel represented, such as agreement on policy and symbolic or active representation across identities.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "future studies should also aim to include a wider array of questions, both related to perceptions of individuals\nwith criminal records and feelings of being represented.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "in terms of policy position, symbolic and active representation on myriad dimensions of identity, etc.), that may be additionally important to explore,",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Racial attitudes predicted several outcomes, but they were measured before treatment. The study cannot show whether either message changed racial attitudes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Most strongly and consistently predictive\nof nearly all outcomes, with exception of the public service motivation of elected officials, was this sample of Democrats’\nracial attitudes.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "racial attitudes were measured as covariates and presented before stimuli;",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The apparent effects on officials’ public-service motives do not survive a Bonferroni correction in either treatment comparison.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Of the above significant outcomes, only the\npublic-serving motives outcome would not be significant after multiple comparisons corrections, particularly Bonferroni.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the above significant outcomes, only the public-serving motives outcome would not be significant after multiple comparisons corrections, particularly Bonferroni.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "Harney extends social categorization theory to campaign advertising: repeatedly identifying a highly salient politician as a group member can make him an exemplar, so judgments about him may carry over to strangers who share the label.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such\nlabels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "theory and evidence would predict greater homogenization and spillover perceptions\nfrom Trump, such that perceptions of individuals with records would be worsened\namongst Democrats after exposure to the advertisement, including its affixed, “convicted criminal” label.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Drawing an analogy to research on welfare and deservingness, Harney distinguishes support for reform from sympathy toward its beneficiaries. Democrats’ less punitive policy preferences need not protect people with records from stigma.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "policy support is distinct from perceptions of individuals who would be directly\nimpacted by these policies. Perhaps the clearest example of this possibility in literature on the dynamics between policy\nsupport and perceptions of its beneficiaries is that of the social safety net.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "In combination with the increased salience and potentially greater reliance on salient members as exemplars,\nsuch as Donald J. Trump, there could still be disconnect between policy support and public perceptions of those who are\nmost immediately impacted by these policies.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The intervention combines perspective-getting with a counterexample to a stereotype. Rather than asking respondents to imagine incarceration, it gives them a legislator’s own account and tests its effect on how they receive an attack ad.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this counter-narrative\nutilized perspective-getting, sharing the experience of a state Assemblymember who was previously incarcerated (i.e., has\na criminal record,) and is dedicated to using their platform to serve the public and advocate for system-impacted populations being represented in government.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "this work seeks to\nunderstand whether providing a counter-stereotype has the potential to counteract negative impacts of propagating such\nlabels.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Harney brings affective and policy-based accounts of representation together: Jackson’s story can elicit empathy while his advocacy signals a policy commitment.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Thus, sharing political messaging highlighting dedicated, system-impacted representatives that may provide both their perspective (i.e., encouraging empathy, positive emotions, and understanding)",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "one that signals their policy alignment (i.e., dedication to justice system reform), would be well-suited to addressing both the affective and policy representation avenues for bolstering support.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Harney offers prospect theory as a possible interpretation, not a demonstrated mechanism: making Trump the reference point could improve ratings of Democratic officials without any change in those officials’ performance.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Collectively, these results may\nsimply be Prospect Theory in action: Donald J. Trump may be serving as reference point that may influence Democrats to see other elected officials as comparatively better [73].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Harney builds on labeling studies by Denver, Ballou and DeWitt; Harris and Socia; and Imhoff. Harney attributes to them the finding that stigmatizing labels produce harsher judgments than person-first descriptions, then asks whether labeling one politician can worsen judgments of other people with records.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Extant literature highlights the causal\nimpact of propagation of labels, such as ‘criminal’, to significantly worsen perceptions\nof individuals with records, relative to more humanizing, person-first language, such\nas ‘a person with a criminal record’‌‌ [8–10].",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "The purpose of this paper was to\nidentify the potential for spillover harm that may have been caused in propagating stigmatizing labels (and/or images)\non Trump, encouraging further inquiry.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
